# NSG Phase 2b — mobility & handover: waypoint paths + 3GPP measurement events.
#
# Supersedes the central claim of mro-layer.patch. See the CORRECTION section.
#
# ── The finding this phase turns on ─────────────────────────────────────────
# LteEnbRrc::TriggerUeAssociationUpdate() — the function that decides every
# automatic mmWave secondary-cell handover — LOST ITS PERIODIC RE-EVALUATION
# upstream. The self-reschedule at the end of it is commented out with a bare
# "//Add flag" (ns-O-RAN-flexric, AbdoKhaled27, 2024-12-08), and m_firstReport
# is only ever reset at cell configuration. Our scenario attaches MC devices
# with AttachToClosestEnb, so m_interRatHoMode stays false and this is the path
# it uses — the InterRat twin, UpdateUeHandoverAssociation(), kept its loop.
#
# Net effect on every run before this patch: automatic handover was evaluated
# ONCE, at t~0, and never again. No speed, margin, TTT or simulation length
# could produce a motion-driven handover, because nothing re-read the
# measurements. E2/xApp-driven handover went through a different path and kept
# working, which is why the handovers counter was never zero and the dead loop
# stayed hidden.
#
# ── CORRECTION to mro-layer.patch (2026-07-18 addendum) ─────────────────────
# That addendum concluded: "MOTION-driven handover churn is unobservable at ANY
# practical horizon of this engine, batch or live." The measurements behind it
# were real, but the cause was misattributed to the horizon and to random-walk
# diffusion. With the evaluation loop restored AND a path that traverses, a
# 300 s /sim/run reaches ~10 s of sim time and produces real A3 handovers on
# four of six UEs. The horizon was never the binding constraint; the dead loop
# was. Diffusion was the second constraint, and waypoints answer it.
#
# ── What is wired (end to end) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# * mobility.waypoints (schema + UI since day one, NEVER passed to ns-3) ->
#   ue_waypoints -> NSG_UE_WAYPOINTS -> --ueWaypointsFile -> a per-UE
#   ns3::WaypointMobilityModel. Format: ';'-sep per UE (aligned with
#   uePositionsFile), '|'-sep 'x,y,z,t' inside a UE, t = arrival seconds.
#   An empty slot keeps that UE on RandomWalk2dOutdoor, and an empty spec makes
#   the run byte-identical to a pre-2b one.
#   - Every UE in a group flies the SAME path TRANSLATED to its own start
#     position, so the group stays spread out. One shared absolute path would
#     pile the group onto a point, which the engine answers with zero DU
#     metrics or an abort (see nsg-nsoran-ue-placement).
#   - mobility.waypoint_mode (new): once | repeat | pingpong, default pingpong.
#     Expanded platform-side to outlast settings.duration_s (cap 200 points).
#     A path walked once ends long before the run does and silently turns a
#     mobility test back into a static one.
#   - Speeds are baked into the waypoint times platform-side; the per-UE
#     ueSpeedsFile lever is skipped for waypoint UEs (a WaypointMobilityModel
#     has no "Speed" attribute and SetAttribute on a missing one is fatal).
# * settings.ho_check_period_ms -> LteEnbRrc::HoCheckPeriodMs. Restores the
#   loop above at a configurable cadence. Engine attribute default 0 (inherited
#   single-shot behaviour); PLATFORM schema default 100 ms — a RAN whose
#   handover logic runs once is a bug, not a baseline, so configs opt out
#   rather than in. This changes results for every existing scenario.
# * settings.ho_a3_entry -> LteEnbRrc::HoA3Entry (schema default true).
#   Requires the 3GPP A3 entry condition Mn > Ms + offset + hysteresis before
#   a TTT handover is scheduled. Upstream scheduled on ANY better neighbour and
#   left the TTT to damp it; HoSinrDifference (ho_sinr_margin_db) is the offset.
# * settings.ho_hysteresis_db -> LteEnbRrc::HoHysteresisDb, added to the offset
#   in both the TTT path and the THRESHOLD path. Default 0 => unchanged.
# * settings.ho_a2_threshold_db -> LteEnbRrc::HoA2ThresholdDb. Serving SINR
#   below it hands over to the best neighbour immediately, bypassing the TTT,
#   with no A3 margin required. Must sit ABOVE OutageThreshold (-5 dB) to be
#   reachable — below that the UE falls back to LTE instead of handing over.
#   Default -1000 = disabled.
# * Each scheduled handover prints one
#   "[NSG-HO] t= imsi= from= to= trigger=A3|A2 diff= serving= ttt=" line to the
#   scenario log, which is what the tests count.
#
# ── Honest limits ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * A WaypointMobilityModel is NOT buildings-aware: it interpolates straight
#   through a building the random walk would have gone around. The channel
#   still computes LOS/NLOS from the real position, so the propagation is
#   right; the trajectory is not. Fine for a drone, wrong for a car in a block.
# * Waypoints need per-UE config positions (uePositionsFile). With the engine's
#   fallback random-disc placement they are ignored, with a log line saying so.
# * Waypoints are clamped to the engine's 100..3900 m area, so a path that runs
#   off the edge is clipped rather than rejected.
# * A reverse leg in pingpong is flown at the speed of the waypoint it starts
#   from (the schema's speed_mps is "speed to reach THIS waypoint").
# * The measurement events are computed on SINR, not RSRP/RSRQ — that is the
#   quantity this engine's secondary-cell handover has always used. A4 and
#   RSRQ-based reselection are not implemented.
# * No fidelity_report.py lever: that harness runs on the :8090 batch path,
#   which reaches ~0.8 s of sim time — 32 m of travel at the 40 m/s ceiling
#   against ~250 m to a cell boundary. Mobility behaviour is not assertable
#   there at all, so the harness only gets the plumbing test. The behavioural
#   proof lives on the /sim/run horizon (test_waypoint_ho_run.py).
#
# ── Measured (2026-07-26) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Geometry: 3 cells in a line 500 m apart, 6 UEs starting 50 m inside the
# cell-1/cell-2 boundary, driving straight across it at 40 m/s.
#
#   SIM HORIZON, measured, per path:
#     :8090 batch (run_scenario.sh, xApp window 40+15/gNB s wall)  ~0.8-0.9 s
#     /sim/run  (live bridge, 300 s wall cap)                      ~9.9 s
#   i.e. ~3.3% of real time for this scenario class (3 gNB / 6 UE / no
#   buildings / no fast fading) — heavier scenes run slower. /sim/run's
#   docstring "frames x indicationPeriodicity sim-seconds" is a CEILING, not a
#   horizon: the run stops at wall_cap_s (900 s) long before 600 frames, so
#   the reachable horizon is ~30 s of sim time, not 150 s.
#
#   LEVER SWEEP, 300 s wall each (engines/nsoran/test_2b_sweep.sh):
#
#     variant             horizon   net m   HO       triggers  engine HO
#     waypoints-loop         9.9s     396   10          A3:10          6
#     waypoints-noloop       9.5s     380    0              -          0
#     walk-loop              9.9s     113   45          A3:45         19
#     hysteresis (+8 dB)     9.1s     364    4           A3:4          4
#     a2 (5 dB)              9.6s     384   28     A2:26,A3:2          8
#
#   * THE LOOP IS THE BLOCKER, cleanly: waypoints-noloop covers the same 380 m
#     of directed motion across the same boundary and produces ZERO handovers.
#     Restoring the loop on that identical run gives 10 decisions / 6 handovers.
#   * Waypoints TRAVERSE: 396 m of net displacement vs 113 m for the random walk
#     at the same 40 m/s. The walk covers similar PATH length and ends up ~a
#     third as far away — that is the diffusion mro-layer.patch measured.
#   * Waypoints are NOT what makes handover possible — the loop is. What they
#     decide is WHERE the UE goes, and the difference shows up in the KIND of
#     handover: the walk mills around the boundary and churns 45 ping-pong
#     decisions / 19 handovers, while the traversing UEs cross once each and
#     move on (10 / 6). A mobility test wanting clean, attributable handovers
#     wants waypoints; a ping-pong / MRO-robustness test can use the walk.
#   * A3 hysteresis is a real damper: +8 dB cuts 10 decisions to 4 on the same
#     motion, which is the anti-ping-pong knob the TTT alone could not provide.
#   * A2 fires and is attributable: at a 5 dB serving-cell floor, 26 of 28
#     decisions carry the A2 trigger and bypass the TTT.
#   * The restored loop costs no measurable simulation speed at 100 ms
#     (3.17% of real time with it off, 3.30% with it on — inside the noise).
